<p>British Prime Minister Keir Starmer told Cabinet on Tuesday he would not resign after Labour lawmakers and several ministers called on him to quit or set a timetable for electing a new party leader later this year. PBS reported that more than 100 Labour lawmakers signed a letter supporting Starmer, while about 90 urged him to set a timetable for leaving. The revolt followed heavy Labour losses in local elections last week. Le Temps reported that the party lost more than 1,400 local elected positions and ground to Reform UK, the Greens and pro-independence parties in Wales and Scotland. Starmer said the government would continue its program, and The Guardian reported that he was due to set out 35 bills in the next parliamentary session covering issues including housing and immigration at Wednesday’s state opening of Parliament. Eleven Labour-affiliated unions, including Unite, Unison and the GMB, were expected to issue a statement saying the party “cannot continue on its current path” and that “at some stage” it will need a plan to elect a new leader.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/y8FAt6HgO4uioCzl1GxWfGz9qI8Y9k06pAbyQ23sgg4nVrSsfboUUAW5OldaXluk3O0uK6kx95kEk1UOYH-Li1XN0dWQiSDv7XwaTy594OtlGU1rjVtKYZzUuYIr7zojnrKfvD7Cn7LCXFHJpsmwheCxRpS_8Gv1-SLkCYNBDXItv0Q_uRa5S6uu5vufKz_Sv8CqzMBzeuKl6Q' alt='Prime Minister Keir Starmer meeting with Labour Party members in London on Friday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Leadership threshold: A Labour leadership contest can begin if the leader resigns or if 20% of Labour MPs nominate a challenger, so a contender currently needs 81 of the party’s 403 MPs.</li><li>No declared challenger: Fast Company reported that, despite MPs calling for Starmer to stand down, no one had yet announced a leadership bid.</li><li>Factors cited: The New York Times cited the economy, the Peter Mandelson appointment controversy and other issues among the problems that weakened Starmer’s leadership.</li></ul><blockquote>The Labour Party has a 